  },
  "articleBody": "When you own a mobile home and rent it out, differentiating between normal wear and tear and tenant-caused damage can be tricky, but it's super important for managing your property effectively. Normal wear and tear includes things like minor scuffs on walls, faded paint, loose door handles from regular use, or minor carpet indentations. These are expected as a property ages and are generally the landlord's responsibility. Tenant-caused damage, on the other hand, goes beyond normal use. This could be large holes in walls, broken windows, missing fixtures, extensive pet damage, or burn marks. These issues typically result from neglect, misuse, or abuse of the property. To make this distinction, always document the property's condition thoroughly before a tenant moves in with photos and videos. Have a detailed move-in checklist signed by both parties. When the tenant moves out, perform another detailed inspection, again with documentation. This comparison is key. If you're unsure, or if the damage requires significant repair like subfloor replacement due to a past leak, a mobile home repair specialist can provide an expert assessment and handle the necessary repairs, ensuring you have the right documentation for any deposit deductions or insurance claims.",
